","sequence":23,"significance":"routine","source":null,"status":null,"text":"In Assembly. Concurrence in Senate amendments pending.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-committee","code":null,"date":"2026-08-25","eventType":"committee_action","historyId":"119124275","location":"Assembly","sequence":23,"significance":"routine","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Re-referred to Com. on ELECTIONS. pursuant to Assembly Rule 77.2.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-other","code":null,"date":"2026-08-26","eventType":"legislative_action","historyId":"119124276","location":"Assembly","sequence":24,"significance":"routine","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Joint Rule 62(a), file notice suspended. (Page 6621.)","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-committee","code":null,"date":"2026-08-28","eventType":"committee_action","historyId":"119124277","location":"Assembly","sequence":25,"significance":"routine","source":null,"status":null,"text":"From committee: That the Senate amendments be concurred in. (Ayes 6. Noes 2.) (August 28).","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-concurred","code":null,"date":"2026-08-30","eventType":"concurred","historyId":"119124278","location":"Assembly","sequence":26,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Urgency clause adopted. Senate amendments concurred in. To Engrossing and Enrolling. (Ayes 61. Noes 17. Page 6853.).","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-enrolled","code":null,"date":"2026-09-09","eventType":"enrolled","historyId":"119124279","location":"Governor","sequence":27,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Enrolled and presented to the Governor at 1 p.m.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-approved","code":null,"date":"2026-09-19","eventType":"approved_by_governor","historyId":"119124280","location":"Governor","sequence":28,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Approved by the Governor.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-chaptered","code":null,"date":"2026-09-19","eventType":"chaptered","historyId":"119124281","location":"Secretary of State","sequence":29,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Chaptered by Secretary of State - Chapter 333, Statutes of 2026.","time":null,"votes":[]}],"affectedStatutes":[{"action":"IS_AMENDED","code":"ELEC","text":"Section 20012 of the Elections Code is amended to read:"}],"approvedDate":"2026-09-19","chapter":{"citation":"Chapter 333, Statutes of 2026","number":"333","type":"CHP","year":"2026"},"chapteredDate":"2026-09-19","committees":[],"conditions":[],"contentHash":"d483610c4a7c2d4436e1af5a3dd39acbc0f95346eb7108d7252b4a1feab78e16","cosponsorCount":null,"digest":"Existing law prohibits a person or entity from knowingly distributing an election communication containing materially deceptive content that portrays specified candidates and officials as doing or saying something that the candidate or official did not do or say if the content is reasonably likely to harm the reputation or electoral prospects of a candidate or is reasonably likely to falsely undermine confidence in the outcome of an election. Existing law applies this prohibition within 120 days of an election in California and, for content involving elections officials and voting machines and related equipment, up to 60 days after an election.\n\nExisting law exempts an election communication from this prohibition if the content includes a disclosure stating that the communication has been manipulated. Existing law requires, for visual media, that the text of this disclosure appear in a size that is easily readable by the average viewer and no smaller than the largest font size of other text appearing in the visual media.\n\nThis bill would instead prohibit the distribution of materially deceptive content that is material to a voter’s electoral decisions or to public confidence in an election’s process or outcome, as specified. The bill would set forth additional requirements relating to the color and font size of the disclosure’s text. The bill would specify that the prohibition applies within the 30 days before an election in California with regard to candidates for any federal, state, or local elected office and elected officials and, with regard to elections officials and voting machines and related equipment, beginning 30 days before an election through 38 days after the election.\n\nUnder existing law, the prohibition does not apply to an advertisement or other election communication containing materially deceptive content that constitutes satire or parody if the communication includes a specified disclosure.\n\nThis bill would also exempt an advertisement or other election communication from these prohibitions if a reasonable person would understand that the content was satire or parody.\n\nExisting law authorizes a recipient of materially deceptive content, among others, to seek an injunction or to bring an action for general or special damages against the person, committee, or other entity that distributed or republished the materially deceptive content.\n\nThis bill would instead authorize the individual depicted in the materially deceptive content to seek the injunction or to bring the action for general or special damages.\n\nThis bill would declare that it is to take effect immediately as an urgency statute.","digestDate":null,"digestVersion":null,"effective":{"basis":"immediate_effect_flag","date":"2026-09-19","note":"The official bill data flags this measure as taking immediate effect. The date shown is the day it was chaptered by the Secretary of State; individual provisions may state other operative dates."},"expiryClauses":[],"firstObservedAt":"2026-11-20T01:12:59Z","id":"ca-20252026-ab502","identifier":"AB 502","indicators":{"Appropriation":"NO","BudgetBill":"NO","Election":"NO","FiscalCommittee":"NO","ImmediateEffect":"YES","LocalProgram":"NO","TaxLevy":"NO","Urgency":"